course: everyday
lesson: 53
title: Feelings and calming down
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Everyone has feelings: happy, sad, angry, scared, worried, excited, jealous, embarrassed, frustrated; all of them are normal, and what matters is what we do with them.
explain: Feelings show in the body: anger can make your face hot, your fists tight and your heart race; fear can give you «butterflies» in your tummy and make you want to run or freeze.
explain: Putting a name on a feeling («I am frustrated because the puzzle will not fit») helps you understand it and makes it easier to calm down and to tell someone what you need.
explain: To calm down when you are angry or scared, stop, breathe in slowly through your nose and out slowly through your mouth a few times, count to ten, move away to a quiet spot, or talk to someone you trust.
explain: A boy in Denver is about to throw his controller when he loses a game; instead he puts it down, takes five slow breaths, and a minute later he can laugh about it, and the controller is not broken.
explain: A boy in Toronto got so angry at his brother that he punched a door; he broke two bones in his hand and spent six weeks in a cast.
explain: A girl in Dublin kept her worries to herself for months; she stopped sleeping and eating well until she finally told her mum, who helped her get support.
fact: All feelings are normal, even the uncomfortable ones.
fact: Having a feeling is never wrong, but hurting someone because of it is.
fact: Anger can make your face hot, your muscles tight and your heart beat fast.
fact: Fear can make your heart race and give you a fluttery feeling in your tummy, as your body gets ready to run or protect itself.
fact: Fear can be useful, because it warns you that something might be dangerous.
fact: Naming a feeling helps you understand it and calm down.
fact: Slow, deep breathing helps the body calm down.
fact: Moving away from what upset you for a few minutes gives you time to cool down.
fact: Feelings change and pass, like weather.
fact: Talking about your feelings with someone you trust often makes you feel better.
fact: Other people have feelings too, and you can often guess them from their face and body.
fact: It is fine to cry when you are sad or hurt; crying is a normal way to let feelings out.
fact: Acting while very angry can lead to hurting yourself, someone else or breaking something you care about.
fact: Keeping big worries hidden for a long time can make you feel ill, so it helps to tell someone you trust.
fact: To fancy someone means to like them in a special way; chanting it at someone is teasing.
fact: When parents say they are going through a rough patch, they mean they are not getting on well; it is not the child's fault, and it is all right to feel sad and to tell a trusted adult.
fact: Someone who is heartbroken is very sad because a boyfriend or girlfriend has ended things; being gentle and giving them space helps.
exercise: Which is true about feelings? | All feelings are normal, but we choose what we do with them | Only happy feelings are normal; the others mean something has gone wrong | Strong children never feel scared | Angry feelings must always be hidden
exercise: Why does slow breathing help you calm down when you are angry? | It slows your body down and gives your mind time to think | It makes the other person go away | It stops all your feelings for the rest of the day, good and bad | It uses up all your anger at once
exercise: How might your body feel when you are angry? | Hot face, tight fists and a fast heartbeat | Cold hands, slow heartbeat and sleepy eyes | A sudden wish to laugh and dance | No different from usual at all
exercise: Why can fear be useful? | It warns you that something might be dangerous | It makes you braver and stronger than everyone else | It helps you fall asleep faster | It is never useful at all
exercise: Why does it help to name a feeling? | It helps you understand it and tell others what you need | It makes the feeling vanish forever | It shows everyone that you are the boss | It stops other people around you from having any feelings of their own
exercise: You feel so angry you want to hit your brother. What is a good first step? | Stop and walk away to cool down | Hit something of his instead to get even | Shout at him as loud as you possibly can | Keep the anger inside and say nothing ever
exercise: How do feelings change over time? | They come and go, like weather | They always stay the same forever | They only change when you sleep | They get stronger and stronger every day
exercise: How can you guess how someone else feels? | By looking at their face and body and listening | By guessing what you would want to eat if you were them | You can never know anything about it | By asking only their parents
exercise: A girl feels a tight knot in her tummy before a spelling test. What feeling is this most likely? | Worry or nervousness | Jealousy | Boredom | Pride
exercise: Why is it wrong to hurt someone because you are angry? | Feeling angry is fine, but hurting is never fine | Because anger is always a bad feeling | Because only adults are ever allowed to feel angry | It is fine if they made you angry first
exercise: Why can talking to a trusted person about a worry help? | They can listen, comfort you and help solve it | They will take the worry and keep it forever | They will tell you worries are silly | It does not help, so it is better to hide worries
exercise: Your friend's dog has died and she is crying. What is the kind thing to do? | Sit with her and say you are sorry | Tell her to stop crying out in public | Tell her she can always get a new dog | Change the subject to something funny
exercise: Is it all right for a boy to cry? | Yes, crying is normal for everyone | No, boys should never cry | Only when he is very small | Only when nobody can see him
exercise: How does a calm-down corner or quiet spot help? | It gives you a break from what upset you | It is a punishment for being upset | It makes the feeling stronger | It means you must stay there on your own for the whole day
test: Leo's sister breaks his model plane. He feels like shouting. Which is the best plan? | Take slow breaths, then tell her calmly how he feels | Break one of her toys so that it is fair and they are even | Pretend he does not care at all | Shout until she cries too
test: Which sentence best names a feeling? | I feel jealous because my friend got the part I wanted | My friend is the worst person in the whole world | Everything is always unfair to me | I do not care about the play anyway
test: Why do your heart and breathing speed up when you are scared? | Your body is getting ready to run or protect itself | Your body is trying to fall asleep | Your heart is tired out from doing too much work all day | Your body is cooling itself down
test: A girl in Phoenix is nervous about her first swimming lesson. What could help her most? | Telling her mum how she feels and taking slow breaths | Pretending to be ill so she can stay home | Telling herself again and again that only babies get nervous | Hiding the feeling so nobody knows
test: What would happen if people never calmed down before acting when they were angry? | They would often do things they later regret | They would always solve their problems much faster | They would never feel angry again | Everyone would be happier at once
test: Your friend's face is red, his fists are tight and he will not look at you. How is he probably feeling? | Angry or upset | Sleepy and relaxed | Happy and excited | Hungry and bored
test: Why is it a good idea to wait until you are calm before solving a quarrel? | When you are calm you can think and listen better | Because the quarrel will solve itself | Because calm people always win | So the other person forgets what happened and you never have to talk about it
test: Which is true? | Feeling sad sometimes is a normal part of life | Sad feelings mean something is wrong with you | Clever children never feel sad | You should always hide sad feelings
test: After counting to ten slowly, Ana still feels very angry. What could she do next? | Go somewhere quiet and talk to a trusted adult | Decide that calming down never works | Throw something soft at the person | Keep counting to a thousand without stopping once
test: so angry i wanna hit him | Step away and breathe slowly | Hit him back, it's totally normal | Shout until you feel loads better | Keep it all inside forever and never tell
test: crying over a film, that's weird right | No, crying is a normal way to let feelings out | Yes, only babies cry at films | Yes, never cry | Only cry when hurt
test: tummy feels all fluttery before the test | That can be fear or nerves, it's normal | You're very ill | You ate a butterfly | It means you'll fail the test, so don't go
test: feel rubbish today, will it stay like this | No, feelings change and pass | Yes, they stay forever and ever | Yes, for a whole year at least | Only if you tell someone about it
test: so angry i wanna smash something | Walk away, breathe slowly and calm down first | Smash something soft quickly | Shout at whoever is near | Punch the wall hard, it's the best way to let it all out
test: been worried for weeks and can't sleep, don't wanna bother anyone | Tell a trusted adult, they want to help | Keep it to yourself so nobody worries | Stay up later until you are tired | Pretend you are fine
test: Ruby says boys shouldn't cry. right? | No, everyone has feelings and crying is normal | Yes, crying is only for girls | Yes, it makes you weak | Yes, unless someone is really badly hurt
test: heard Sam threw a chair in class cos he was so angry, they're saying he's dangerous now | Feelings are okay but hurting is not; do not spread the label | It proves anger is a bad feeling to have | It means Sam must be dangerous forever | It is fine to spread because a chair was involved and someone could have been hurt
test: my mate says boys aren't allowed to cry, it's weak | Wrong; all feelings are normal, and crying is a natural way to show them | He is right, crying is only for girls | He is right, boys should hide sadness | He is right, crying only makes your sad feelings worse and lasts much longer
test: Grace had a meltdown at sports day and someone filmed it, should i watch and send it on | No, sharing it would hurt her; she needs kindness, not an audience | Yes, because it already exists | Yes, so she sees how she looked | Yes, but only to a few people who weren't at sports day and missed it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises feelings? | All feelings are normal, though harming another because of them is wrong | Some feelings are forbidden and must never be felt by a well-behaved child | Uncomfortable feelings indicate illness | Feelings remain unchanged for life
exam: Identify a method by which the body may be calmed. | Slow and deep breathing | Shouting as loudly as possible | Running out into the busy road | Holding the breath for a minute
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning fear. | Fear may be useful, warning that something might be dangerous | Fear serves no purpose whatsoever | Fear is felt only by young children | Fear always signifies that one is unwell and requires a doctor
exam: It being given that a child is upset by an event, determine a measure that affords time to calm down. | Moving away from the cause for a few minutes | Remaining as close as possible to the cause | Hitting a nearby object | Refusing to name the feeling
test: they all chanting "Jack fancies Emily" n my face went red, so cross rn | They are teasing that Jack likes Emily; breathe, walk away, tell an adult if it goes on | It means Jack is ill, so go to the nurse | It means you must hit the loudest one | It means Emily is angry with Jack, so shout back louder than all of them together
test: mum n dad keep saying they're "going through a rough patch", i keep crying at night | They are not getting on well; it is ok to feel sad, so tell them or a trusted adult | It means the road outside is bumpy | It means they will leave you, so hide your feelings | It means you caused it, so be perfect from now on and they will be happy again
test: auntie said my big sis is "heartbroken" since her boyfriend dumped her, she slammed the door on me lol | Her boyfriend ended it and she is very sad; be gentle and give her some space | Her heart is really broken, so call an ambulance | She is angry at you, so slam your door back even harder | It is funny, so tease her about it
